In this option, the company is being honest about Liu's health condition (he took a medical leave due to liver cancer). Therefore, the principle of utmost good faith is being followed.

B) Mr. Zhang, in good health, has purchased a simple life insurance under the collective organization of the unit, and fills in the word "health" in the column "health" of the insured on the insurance application form.

Here, Mr. Zhang is in good health and truthfully reports his health status as "health." This is an example of fulfilling the principle of utmost good faith.

C) In the new semester, the school asked freshmen to pay an insurance premium of 25 yuan each, of which student safety insurance was 10 yuan, and additional disease insurance was 15 yuan. On September 8, a new student Zhang had abdominal pain and was diagnosed with a congenital disease by the hospital. Zhang was hospitalized, and Zhang's father asked the insurance company to reimburse the medical expenses.

In this option, it is not explicitly stated that Zhang concealed any information about his health condition when applying for the insurance. The fact that he was diagnosed with a congenital disease after the insurance was purchased does not necessarily imply a breach of the principle of utmost good faith unless he was aware of the condition beforehand and did not disclose it. Since the context does not provide information suggesting that Zhang concealed any information about his health, we cannot conclude that he breached the principle of utmost good faith based on the information given.

D) Li was hospitalized with gastric cancer, was discharged after the operation, and went to work normally. Later, Li was introduced by someone to insure for simple life insurance, and when filling out the insurance form, he did not declare the fact that he was suffering from cancer.

In this option, Li had a history of gastric cancer and did not disclose this information when applying for the insurance. This is a clear breach of the principle of utmost good faith because he concealed a significant health condition that could affect the insurance risk.

Therefore, the option where the applicant has not fulfilled the principle of utmost good faith is:

D) Li was hospitalized with gastric cancer, was discharged after the operation, and went to work normally. Later, Li was introduced by someone to insure for simple life insurance, and when filling out the insurance form, he did not declare the fact that he was suffering from cancer.

So the correct answer is D.
