output:  To approach this logical reasoning problem, let's break down the given context and the question.

The context states: "The literary works of the Chinese writer Mo Yan have made the hidden Chinese vernacular concerned by the readers of the world and recognized by the international literary world, which has once again proved that this judgment is that only the national is the world."

This statement can be translated into a logical form. The key phrase here is "only the national is the world." This can be interpreted as: "Something is world-renowned only if it is national." In logical terms, this translates to: "If something is world-renowned, then it must be national." This can be written as: 

\[ \text{World} \implies \text{National} \]

Or in other words, if something is part of the world (world-renowned), then it must also be national.

Now let's evaluate each of the options given:

A) It's national, it's world.
This translates to: If it is national, then it is world-renowned. In logical terms, this is: 

\[ \text{National} \implies \text{World} \]

B) If it is not national, it is not world.
This translates to: If something is not national, then it is not world-renowned. In logical terms, this is the contrapositive of the statement \(\text{World} \implies \text{National}\). The contrapositive of \(P \implies Q\) is \(\neg Q \implies \neg P\). So the contrapositive of \(\text{World} \implies \text{National}\) is:

\[ \neg \text{National} \implies \neg \text{World} \]

This can be read as "If it is not national, it is not world-renowned."

C) As long as the nation is, it is the world.
This is similar to option A and translates to: If it is national, then it is world-renowned. In logical terms, this is:

\[ \text{National} \implies \text{World} \]

D) Only the world is national.
This statement is a bit ambiguous but can be interpreted as "Something is national only if it is world-renowned." In logical terms, this translates to:

\[ \text{National} \implies \text{World} \]

Now let's compare these options to the original statement \(\text{World} \implies \text{National}\).

Option A and C both say \(\text{National} \implies \text{World}\), which is not the same as the original statement \(\text{World} \implies \text{National}\).

Option B says \(\neg \text{National} \implies \neg \text{World}\), which is the contrapositive of \(\text{World} \implies \text{National}\). Since a statement and its contrapositive are logically equivalent, Option B is logically equivalent to the original statement.

Option D says \(\text{National} \implies \text{World}\), which is the same as Option A and C and is not the same as the original statement.

Therefore, the correct option is B.

So the correct answer is:

\[ \boxed{B} \]
